{"task_id": "Mbpp/4", "solution": "```python\ndef heap_queue_largest(sequence, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n largest integers from a sequence and returns them in descending order.\n    This implementation uses sorting due to critical constraints against external modules.\n    \"\"\"\n    # CRITICAL REQUIREMENT: \"Do NOT add isinstance() checks, type guards, or raise exceptions for edge cases. Trust the caller's inputs.\"\n    # CRITICAL REQUIREMENT: \"Do NOT call helper functions or utilities that are not defined in the same code block. Every function you call must be defined inline in your response.\"\n    # Given the above, importing and using 'heapq' is forbidden.\n    # The simplest, most direct logic without heapq, and without validation, is to sort the entire sequence.\n\n    if n <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Trusting 'sequence' is an iterable of integers as per critical requirements.\n    # Convert to a list to ensure it's mutable for sorting and handles non-reusable iterators.\n    lst_sequence = list(sequence)\n\n    if not lst_sequence:\n        return []\n\n    # Sort the list in descending order.\n    lst_sequence.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    # Return the first n elements. Python slicing handles cases where n is larger than the list length.\n    return lst_sequence[:n]\n```"}
