{"task_id": "Mbpp/4", "solution": "import heapq\n\ndef _select_top_n(numbers: list, n: int) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Selects the n largest elements using a min-heap of size n.\n    This internal helper processes the list to find the top N.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers or n <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    min_heap = []\n    for number in numbers:\n        if len(min_heap) < n:\n            heapq.heappush(min_heap, number)\n        elif number > min_heap[0]:\n            heapq.heapreplace(min_heap, number)\n    return min_heap\n\ndef heap_queue_largest(numbers: list, n: int) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n largest integers from a list, returned in descending order.\n    Handles unspecified edge cases: n <= 0 returns []; n >= len(numbers) returns all sorted descending.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    # If n is greater than or equal to the total number of elements, return all elements sorted descending.\n    if n >= len(numbers):\n        return sorted(numbers, reverse=True)\n\n    # Use the helper to select the top n elements, maintaining them in a min-heap.\n    selected_elements = _select_top_n(numbers, n)\n\n    # Sort the selected elements in descending order for the final result.\n    selected_elements.sort(reverse=True)\n    return selected_elements"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/2", "solution": "def similar_elements(seqA, seqB):\n    \"\"\"Return a list of distinct elements present in both seqA and seqB using value equality (==).\n    The result order is based on the first appearance of elements in seqA.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    for a in seqA:\n        for b in seqB:\n            if a == b:\n                # Linear search for existing equals in result\n                already = False\n                for r in result:\n                    if r == a:\n                        already = True\n                        break\n                if not already:\n                    result.append(a)\n                break  # Move to the next element in seqA once a match for 'a' is found\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/3", "solution": "```python\n# src/__init__.py\n# Package marker for the 'src' directory.\n```\n```python\n# src/models.py\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Number:\n    \"\"\"Represents an integer value for prime checking.\"\"\"\n    value: int\n\n@dataclass\nclass FunctionResult:\n    \"\"\"Stores the result of the is_not_prime evaluation.\"\"\"\n    evaluated_for: int\n    is_not_prime: bool\n```\n```python\n# src/prime_logic.py\nfrom src.models import Number # Imported for typing/clarity as specified in design notes.\n\nclass PrimeCheckLogic:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates the algorithm for checking if a number is prime.\"\"\"\n\n    def is_prime(self, n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Return True if n is prime, False otherwise.\"\"\"\n        if n < 2:\n            return False\n        if n == 2:\n            return True\n        if n % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n        i = 3\n        while i * i <= n:\n            if n % i == 0:\n                return False\n            i += 2\n        return True\n```\n```python\n# src/is_not_prime.py\nfrom src.prime_logic import PrimeCheckLogic\nfrom src.models import FunctionResult # Imported as specified in design dependencies.\n\nclass IsNotPrimeFunction:\n    \"\"\"Adapter/service layer exposing the is_not_prime use-case.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        \"\"\"Initializes with the prime check logic.\"\"\"\n        self._logic = PrimeCheckLogic()\n\n    def is_not_prime(self, n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Determines if a number is not prime based on primality and n < 2 rule.\"\"\"\n        if n < 2:\n            return True  # Integers less than 2 are considered not prime.\n        return not self._logic.is_prime(n)\n\ndef is_not_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Public API function: returns True if n is not prime, False if prime.\"\"\"\n    return IsNotPrimeFunction().is_not_prime(n)\n```\n```python\n# tests/test_is_not_prime.py\nfrom src.is_not_prime import is_not_prime\n\nclass TestCase:\n    \"\"\"A simple test case class for the is_not_prime function.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, id: int, input: int, expected: bool):\n        \"\"\"Initializes the test case with an ID, input, and expected result.\"\"\"\n        self.id = id\n        self.input = input\n        self.expected = expected\n\n    def run(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Runs the test case and returns True if the assertion passes.\"\"\"\n        return is_not_prime(self.input) == self.expected\n\n# Required unit test assertion per SRS / NFR:\nassert is_not_prime(2) == False\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/6", "solution": "```python\n# src/differ_at_one_bit_pos.py\n# Python 3 utility for checking if two integers differ by exactly one bit.\n\ndef xor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Computes the bitwise XOR of two integers.\n    This helper function performs the `a ^ b` operation.\"\"\"\n    return a ^ b\n\ndef is_single_bit(x: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if an integer has exactly one bit set.\n    It returns True if x is a power of two (and not zero), False otherwise.\"\"\"\n    return x != 0 and (x & (x - 1)) == 0\n\ndef differ_At_One_Bit_Pos(a: int, b: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Determines if two integers differ in exactly one bit position.\n    It returns True if their XOR sum has exactly one bit set, False otherwise.\"\"\"\n    # Input validation and error raising (e.g., TypeError) are excluded here\n    # as per critical requirements overriding the design specification.\n    \n    xor_result = xor(a, b)\n    return is_single_bit(xor_result)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/7", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef tokenize_words(s: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Tokenize using whitespace splitting.\n    Returns list of token substrings preserving original casing and punctuation as-is.\n    \"\"\"\n    return s.split()\n\ndef find_char_long(input: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Tokenize input, filter to tokens with len >= 4, preserve casing, and return list of tokens.\n    Uses whitespace splitting for tokenization as documented.\n    \"\"\"\n    tokens = tokenize_words(input)\n    filtered = [t for t in tokens if len(t) >= 4]\n    return filtered\n```"}
