{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "```python\ndef search(input_list: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return the unique element from a sorted list where all other elements appear twice.\n    This uses an O(log n) binary search approach.\"\"\"\n    low = 0\n    high = len(input_list) - 1\n\n    while low < high:\n        mid = low + (high - low) // 2\n\n        # Ensure 'mid' points to the start of a potential pair (an even index).\n        # If 'mid' is odd, decrement it to make it an even index.\n        if mid % 2 == 1:\n            mid -= 1\n\n        # If elements at 'mid' and 'mid + 1' are equal, they form a pair.\n        # The unique element must be in the array segment after this pair.\n        if input_list[mid] == input_list[mid + 1]:\n            low = mid + 2\n        # Otherwise, the unique element is either at 'mid' or somewhere before it.\n        # The search space is narrowed to the left half including 'mid'.\n        else:\n            high = mid\n\n    # When the loop terminates, 'low' (and 'high') points to the index of the unique element.\n    return input_list[low]\n```"}
