{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef is_perfect_square(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a non-negative integer is a perfect square.\n    Returns True if n is an integer k*k for some integer k.\"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        return False\n    # Calculate the integer part of the square root.\n    root = int(math.sqrt(n))\n    # Check if squaring this integer root yields the original number.\n    return root * root == n\n\ndef ceil_sqrt(n: float | int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Computes the integer ceiling of the square root of n.\n    The input 'n' is assumed to be non-negative.\"\"\"\n    return int(math.ceil(math.sqrt(n)))\n\ndef next_Perfect_Square(number: float | int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Returns the smallest integer perfect square strictly greater than the provided numeric input.\n    The input 'number' is assumed to be non-negative.\"\"\"\n    # Compute an initial candidate k by taking the ceiling of the square root of the input number.\n    k = ceil_sqrt(number)\n    # If k*k is less than or equal to the number, the current k*k is not strictly greater.\n    # Therefore, we need to increment k to find the next perfect square.\n    if k * k <= number:\n        k += 1\n    # Return the perfect square k*k, which is the smallest perfect square strictly greater than 'number'.\n    return k * k\n```"}
