{"task_id": "Mbpp/296", "solution": "def _count_by_merge_sort(arr):\n    \"\"\"Recursively sorts an array and counts inversions during the merge step.\n    Returns a tuple of the sorted list and the total inversion count.\"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return arr, 0\n\n    mid = n // 2\n    left_half, left_inv = _count_by_merge_sort(arr[:mid])\n    right_half, right_inv = _count_by_merge_sort(arr[mid:])\n\n    merged_arr = []\n    inversions = left_inv + right_inv\n    i = j = 0\n    while i < len(left_half) and j < len(right_half):\n        if left_half[i] <= right_half[j]:\n            merged_arr.append(left_half[i])\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            # If right_half[j] is smaller than left_half[i], it forms inversions\n            # with all remaining elements in left_half.\n            merged_arr.append(right_half[j])\n            inversions += (len(left_half) - i)\n            j += 1\n\n    merged_arr.extend(left_half[i:])\n    merged_arr.extend(right_half[j:])\n\n    return merged_arr, inversions\n\ndef get_Inv_Count(input_list):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the number of inversions in the input list.\n    It uses a merge-sort based algorithm to count inversions.\"\"\"\n    # Per critical requirements, type checking and exception raising are forbidden.\n    # The _validate_input helper described in design is therefore omitted.\n    _, count = _count_by_merge_sort(list(input_list))\n    return count"}
