{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef _validate_input(integers: list) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Ensures the input list has at least two elements, raising ValueError if not.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(integers, list) or len(integers) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list must contain at least two elements.\")\n\ndef _find_two_largest(integers: list) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"Identifies the two largest numeric values from distinct indices and returns them (smaller, larger).\"\"\"\n    max1 = -math.inf\n    max2 = -math.inf\n    for x in integers:\n        if x > max1:\n            max2 = max1\n            max1 = x\n        elif x > max2:\n            max2 = x\n    return (min(int(max1), int(max2)), max(int(max1), int(max2)))\n\ndef _find_two_smallest(integers: list) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"Identifies the two smallest numeric values from distinct indices and returns them (smaller, larger).\"\"\"\n    min1 = math.inf\n    min2 = math.inf\n    for x in integers:\n        if x < min1:\n            min2 = min1\n            min1 = x\n        elif x < min2:\n            min2 = x\n    return (min(int(min1), int(min2)), max(int(min1), int(min2)))\n\ndef _choose_best_pair(pair_a: tuple[int, int], pair_b: tuple[int, int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"Compares products of two candidate pairs and returns the pair with the larger product.\"\"\"\n    product_a = pair_a[0] * pair_a[1]\n    product_b = pair_b[0] * pair_b[1]\n    if product_a >= product_b:\n        return pair_a\n    else:\n        return pair_b\n\ndef max_Product(integers: list) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns two elements from the input list (distinct indices) whose product is maximal.\n    Raises ValueError if the input list has fewer than two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_input(integers)\n\n    largest_pair = _find_two_largest(integers)\n    smallest_pair = _find_two_smallest(integers)\n\n    return _choose_best_pair(largest_pair, smallest_pair)\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/419", "solution": "```python\n# src/round_and_sum.py\n# Domain value classes for clarity, as per design.\nclass InputList:\n    \"\"\"A conceptual container for the input list.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, raw_list: list):\n        self.values = [NumericValue(val, i) for i, val in enumerate(raw_list)]\n        self.length = len(raw_list)\n\nclass NumericValue:\n    \"\"\"Wraps an input element and its index.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, original_value: float, index: int):\n        self.original_value = original_value\n        self.index = index\n\nclass RoundedValue:\n    \"\"\"Holds the integer result of rounding a NumericValue.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: int):\n        self.value = value\n\nclass Result:\n    \"\"\"Holds the final integer product.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: int):\n        self.value = value\n\ndef round_and_sum(numbers: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Rounds numbers, sums them, and multiplies by the list length.\n    Rounds .5 values up towards positive infinity.\n    \"\"\"\n    input_list = InputList(numbers)\n    \n    # Return 0 for an empty list, a reasonable default.\n    if input_list.length == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    rounded_integers = []\n    for numeric_value_wrapper in input_list.values:\n        num = numeric_value_wrapper.original_value\n        \n        # Custom rounding logic: round half up towards positive infinity.\n        # This emulates math.floor(num + 0.5) using only built-in operations.\n        val_pre_floor = num + 0.5\n        \n        # Emulate floor operation (truncates towards negative infinity).\n        if val_pre_floor >= 0 or val_pre_floor == int(val_pre_floor):\n            rounded_val = int(val_pre_floor)\n        else:\n            rounded_val = int(val_pre_floor) - 1\n            \n        rounded_integers.append(RoundedValue(rounded_val))\n\n    total_sum = sum(rv.value for rv in rounded_integers)\n    \n    product = total_sum * input_list.length\n    \n    final_result = Result(product)\n    return final_result.value\n```\n```python\n# tests/test_round_and_sum.py\nfrom src.round_and_sum import round_and_sum\n\n# Test for the SRS-provided example assertion.\nassert round_and_sum([22.4, 4.0, -16.22, -9.10, 11.00, -12.22, 14.20, -5.20, 17.50]) == 243\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/419", "solution": "def _round_value(value: float) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Rounds a numeric value to the nearest integer, using round half away from zero for ties.\n    This means positive .5 values round up and negative .5 values round down in magnitude.\"\"\"\n    if value >= 0:\n        return int(value + 0.5)\n    else:\n        return int(value - 0.5)\n\ndef _sum_rounded(rounded_values: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Computes the sum of a list of integers.\n    This helper aggregates the rounded numeric values.\"\"\"\n    return sum(rounded_values)\n\ndef _print_result(multiplied_total: int) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Prints the final multiplied total to standard output.\n    This fulfills the side-effect requirement of the system.\"\"\"\n    print(multiplied_total)\n\ndef round_and_sum(numbers: list[float]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Rounds numbers, sums them, multiplies by list length, prints, and returns the total.\n    It applies \"round half away from zero\" for tie-breaking as specified in the design.\"\"\"\n    \n    # Input validation, as specified in the design, is omitted here\n    # due to the critical requirement forbidding isinstance() checks and exceptions.\n    # The Result dataclass is also omitted due to the \"no class wrapper\" critical requirement.\n\n    rounded_values = [_round_value(num) for num in numbers]\n    sum_of_rounded = _sum_rounded(rounded_values)\n    multiplied_total = sum_of_rounded * len(numbers)\n\n    _print_result(multiplied_total)\n\n    return multiplied_total"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/420", "solution": "```python\n\"\"\"\nProvides the cube_Sum function as specified in the SRS.\nComputes the sum of cubes of the first n even natural numbers.\n\"\"\"\n\ndef cube_Sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes and returns the sum of cubes of the first n even natural numbers (2, 4, ..., 2n).\n    Returns an integer result.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum((2 * k)**3 for k in range(1, n + 1))\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/420", "solution": "```python\ndef cube_Sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the sum of cubes of the first n even natural numbers using a closed-form formula.\n    Assumes n is a positive integer for computation based on design context.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Computation using the closed-form formula: 2 * n^2 * (n+1)^2\n    result = 2 * (n ** 2) * ((n + 1) ** 2)\n    return result\n```"}
