{"task_id": "Mbpp/726", "solution": "```python\n# src/multiply_elements.py\n\n\"\"\"\nModule for the Multiply Elements Function API.\n\nThis API provides a pure, deterministic function `multiply_elements` that computes\nthe product of adjacent elements in an input tuple.\nIt validates input for type, length, and numeric-ness, raising ValueError or TypeError as specified.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Tuple, Union\n\n# Define 'number' type as specified in the design.\nnumber = Union[int, float]\n\n@dataclass\nclass Element:\n    \"\"\"Represents a numeric value and its 1-based position.\"\"\"\n    value: number\n    position: int\n\n@dataclass\nclass InputTuple:\n    \"\"\"Typed representation of the input tuple with a convenience length property.\"\"\"\n    elements: Tuple[number, ...]\n\n    @property\n    def length(self) -> int:\n        return len(self.elements)\n\n@dataclass\nclass OutputTuple:\n    \"\"\"Typed representation of the returned tuple with a convenience length property.\"\"\"\n    elements: Tuple[number, ...]\n\n    @property\n    def length(self) -> int:\n        return len(self.elements)\n\nclass Validator:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates validation logic for input tuples.\"\"\"\n\n    def validateLength(self, input_tuple: tuple) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Ensures input is a tuple and has at least 2 elements; raises ValueError on failure.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(input_tuple, tuple):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be an ordered tuple.\")\n        if len(input_tuple) < 2:\n            raise ValueError(\"Input tuple must contain at least 2 elements.\")\n\n    def validateNumericElements(self, input_tuple: tuple) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Ensures every element is numeric (int or float); raises TypeError on failure.\"\"\"\n        for element in input_tuple:\n            if not isinstance(element, (int, float)):\n                raise TypeError(\"All elements must be numeric (integer or floating-point).\")\n\ndef multiply_elements(input_tuple: Tuple[number, ...]) -> Tuple[number, ...]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the product of adjacent elements in an input tuple.\n    Raises ValueError for insufficient length or TypeError for non-numeric elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    validator = Validator()\n    validator.validateLength(input_tuple)\n    validator.validateNumericElements(input_tuple)\n\n    # Compute adjacent products as specified.\n    products = tuple(input_tuple[i] * input_tuple[i+1] for i in range(len(input_tuple)-1))\n    return products\n```\n```python\n# src/__init__.py\n\n\"\"\"\nPackage initializer for the src module.\n\nRe-exports the multiply_elements function for convenient access.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom .multiply_elements import multiply_elements\n\n__all__ = [\"multiply_elements\"]\n```\n```python\n# tests/test_multiply_elements.py\n\n\"\"\"\nUnit tests for the multiply_elements function, including the example acceptance test.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom src.multiply_elements import multiply_elements\n\ndef test_example_acceptance():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verifies the canonical example acceptance test for multiply_elements.\n    Asserts multiply_elements((1, 5, 7, 8, 10)) returns (5, 35, 56, 80).\n    \"\"\"\n    assert multiply_elements((1, 5, 7, 8, 10)) == (5, 35, 56, 80)\n```"}
