{"task_id": "Mbpp/809", "solution": "```python\ndef check_smaller(tuple1: tuple, tuple2: tuple) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Compares elements of `tuple2` to `tuple1` element-wise using `tuple2[i] < tuple1[i]`.\n    Returns True if all comparisons are true, otherwise False; raises ValueError if tuples have different lengths.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(tuple1) != len(tuple2):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input tuples must be of equal length.\")\n\n    return all(tuple2[i] < tuple1[i] for i in range(len(tuple1)))\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/736", "solution": "def binary_search_left(seq, value) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the leftmost insertion point using binary search.\n    Assumes seq is non-empty and value is within its bounds for typical use.\n    \"\"\"\n    low = 0\n    high = len(seq)\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if seq[mid] < value:\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:  # seq[mid] >= value\n            high = mid\n    return low\n\ndef left_insertion(seq, value) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Returns the leftmost insertion index for value in a sorted sequence.\n    Handles boundary cases and uses binary search for general cases.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(seq)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    if value < seq[0]:\n        return 0\n    \n    if value > seq[-1]:\n        return n\n    \n    return binary_search_left(seq, value)"}
